WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.252
This query uses CTEs and a window function.

00:00:03.252 --> 00:00:07.607
The monthly sales stage has one row per month and product.

00:00:07.607 --> 00:00:12.357
The ranked stage keeps that grain and adds a rank within each month.

00:00:12.357 --> 00:00:16.092
The final SELECT keeps the top two rank groups.

00:00:16.092 --> 00:00:21.302
This is a good example of readable staged SQL that can transfer to Spark SQL.

00:00:21.302 --> 00:00:26.547
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:30.957
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:35.337
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.337 --> 00:00:41.277
In a real setting such as a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files,

00:00:41.277 --> 00:00:47.212
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.212 --> 00:00:49.402
before discussing syntax.

00:00:49.402 --> 00:00:55.297
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
